Here, we provide a facile, cost-efficient, and environmentally friendly approach for reversible digestion/regeneration of $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs by use of bicarbonate or carbonate solutions. UiO-66, a classical $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF consisting of zirconium-oxo cluster and 1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC), is selected to complete the digestion–regeneration circle. On the other hand, ligand recycling and Pd nanocatalyst synthesis can be achieved from ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{PCN-}}$ 224 (palladium nanoparticles are embedded in PCN-224, which is a $\mathsf{Z r-M O F},$ ) based on this digestion approach. In addition, a digestion mechanism actuated by entropy change is discussed based on the formation of dissoluble complexes $\mathrm{[}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{CO}_{3})_{4}]^{2-}$ for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF digestion process.
## Results and discussion
Mechanism of $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -MOF digestion. Based on the above discussion, a MOF digestion–regeneration cycle was promoted with a view to zirconium species evolution (Fig. 3c). First, zirconiumoxo clusters, $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}]}$ , exist in UiO-66 as metal sites. After adding $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O_{3}}$ aqueous solution, the soluble AZC is formed instead of zirconium-based clusters. Then, through the treatment of acetic acid and evaporation processes, UiO-66-R is emerged in DMF with the benzoic acid as a modulator via solvothermal method. Figure 3d shows a hypothetical competition–digestion mechanism of UiO-66. Like the deterioration mechanism of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{OH^{-41}}$ , zirconium-oxo cluster is subject to the attack of $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}{}^{-}$ , which takes place of the ligands coordinating with zirconium atoms. The ligands with carboxyl groups transfer into the corresponding salts; meanwhile, small molecules such as $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are generated. Because the structures of AZC are various and sensitive to the environment44, we denoted herein the anions of AZC by $\mathrm{[{Zr}_{2}(O H)_{2}(C O_{3})_{4}]^{2-}}$ based on the above results42. Thus, the chemical reaction for UiO-66 digestion could be written as
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(B D C)_{6}}+18\mathrm{HCO_{3}^{-}}\rightarrow3\left[\mathrm{Zr_{2}(O H)_{2}(C O_{3})_{4}}\right]^{2-}}\\ {+8\mathrm{H_{2}O}+6\mathrm{CO_{2}}+6\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}.}\end{array}
$$
